#--: Overview

The declarations live in #~"lib/system/thread.z", each with its own doc
comment; this page is the prose and the worked examples.

The #~"thread" unit runs work on its own operating-system thread. The work is a
#b:"task": a class that conforms to #~"(thread.Task R)" -- or #~"(thread.TaskVal
r)" when its result is a value type -- whose #~"call" answers the result. The
task is #b:"moved" to the thread when it starts, so nothing else can touch it
while it runs; it is destroyed #b:"on that thread" as soon as #~"call"
returns, and #~"join" answers what #~"call" answered:

#code language=zerolang name=counter: """
  # Counter -- counts on its own thread
  Counter: class {
      # where it starts
      n: i64
  } as {
      task: thread.TaskVal i64
      call: function {:this} out i64 is {
          i: 0.i64
          for i < 1000 loop {
              this.n = this.n + 1
              i = i + 1
          }
          return this.n
      }
  }

  main: function is {
      h: thread.spawnVal task: (Counter n: 0) name: "counter"
      print "counted \{h.join}"
  }
"""

A task with no result is a #~"(thread.Task null)" whose #~"call" declares no
#~"out"; its #~"join" answers nothing and waits.

There are no shared mutable variables to protect by hand: what a thread uses is
either moved to it or borrowed by it, and the lock model below keeps the owner
away from what a running task borrows. The #~"sync" unit (#~"Mutex",
the channel ends #~"Receiver" and #~"Sender", and the rest) is for data that
really is shared.

#--: Tasks and handles

#ul:
  [#*: #b:"Task R" -- #~"protocol { call: function {:this} out R }", R a
    reference type (#~"null" for no result); #b:"TaskVal r" its twin for a
    value-type result. The thread's body; it runs once. A task is any class
    that declares one (#~"task: thread.Task String").]
  [#*: #b:"spawn" -- #~"function {task: T.take name: \"\" stackSize: 0} outx
    (JoinHandle R)", for a #~"(Task R)"; #b:"spawnVal" the same for a
    #~"(TaskVal r)", answering a #~"JoinHandleVal r". R is inferred from the
    task's conformance. Starts the task. #~"name" labels the thread for
    debuggers and #~"top" (15 bytes are kept); a #~"stackSize" of #~"0" is the
    default, 8 MiB -- the main thread's usual size, so a program that recurses
    deeply on main recurses as deeply on a thread.]
  [#*: #b:"JoinHandle R" / #b:"JoinHandleVal r" -- the started thread, typed
    by its result alone, so one list may hold the handles of different tasks.
    #~"join" waits for it and answers the result; #~"isFinished" says whether
    #~"join" would wait; #~"id" is the thread's id as the operating system
    numbers it (#~"0" once joined). #b:"Destroying a handle that was never
    joined joins it": a scope cannot end while a thread it started still uses
    what the scope owns.]
  [#*: #b:"spawnDetached" -- start a #~"(Task R)" with no handle: the thread
    runs to its end on its own and destroys its task and its result. The
    program may end while it still runs.]
  [#*: #b:"yieldNow" -- let another thread run.]
  [#*: #b:"running" -- how many threads the program started that have not
    finished. A thread leaves the count after its task is destroyed, so a
    program that must not end before its detached tasks waits for #~"0":
    #~"for thread.running > 0 loop thread.yieldNow".]

Read bare, a value whose type has a #~"call" is #b:"called" -- a task is an
iterator-shaped value in that respect -- so a task held in a variable is passed
with #~".take": #~"thread.spawn task: t.take".

#--: Borrowing tasks

A task may hold borrows -- a #~".view" or #~".borrow" field into data its
owner keeps. The handle carries the task's locks (#~"spawn" is a native
#~"outx" function, the one kind of call that may keep a moved value's locks),
so while the handle lives the owner can neither write nor read nor drop what
the task borrows, and two running tasks cannot write one source:

#code language=zerolang name=summer: """
  # Summer -- sums a list its owner lends it
  Summer: class {
      # the borrowed input
      xs: (ListVal i64).view
  } as {
      task: thread.TaskVal i64
      call: function {:this} out i64 is {
          total: 0.i64
          for each x: this.xs loop total = total + x
          return total
      }
  }

  main: function is {
      xs: ListVal i64
      xs.append 1
      xs.append 2
      {
          h: thread.spawnVal task: (Summer :xs)
          print "summed \{h.join}"
      }
      xs.append 3
      print "the owner writes again: \{xs.length}"
  }
"""

Inside the inner block #~"xs.append 3" is refused (the handle holds a lock on
#~"xs"); after it, the lock is gone.

#~"spawnDetached" is #~"out", not #~"outx": nothing would keep what a detached
task borrows alive, so a task holding a borrow is refused there.

#--: What may cross to a thread

A task moves to its thread whole, with everything it holds. A type that
conforms to #~"thread.ThreadBound" -- a marker protocol, with no methods --
never makes that move: a value of it is refused as a task, and as anything a
task holds at any depth, whether it is owned, viewed or borrowed, or sits in a
list inside another class -- at #~"spawn", #~"spawnVal" and #~"spawnDetached"
alike -- and so is a task whose result holds one, which #~"join" would hand
back across threads. The lock guards (#~"sync.MutexGuard",
#~"sync.ReadGuard", #~"sync.WriteGuard") are the stdlib's: a guard unlocks on
the thread that locked, and unlocked on another the lock is undefined. Lock on
the new thread instead -- the task holds a handle to the Mutex (#~"m.share")
and takes the guard itself:

#code language=zerolang expect=E0200: """
  # Waiter -- waits on a guard its owner locked
  Waiter: class {
      # the guard, locked on the owner's thread
      g: sync.MutexGuard (ListVal i64)
  } as {
      task: thread.Task null
      call: function {:this} is {
          print "\{this.g.value.length}"
      }
  }

  main: function is {
      m: (sync.Mutex (ListVal i64)).create value: ListVal i64
      g: m.lock
      h: thread.spawn task: (Waiter :g)
      h.join
  }
"""

Refused: "Cannot hand 'Waiter' to another thread with #~"thread.spawn": it
holds 'MutexGuard', and a #~"thread.ThreadBound" value never leaves the thread
that made it".

A task is the only way a value reaches another thread. A channel end, a Mutex
handle and whatever they carry go over inside one, so a Sender of guards is
refused where the task holding it is spawned, by the same check: the check
reads a type's type arguments as part of what it holds.

What makes a call a crossing is its parameter, not its name: a value moved
into a parameter typed or bounded by a protocol that conforms to
#~"thread.Crossing" is handed to another thread, and is checked there.
#~"Task" and #~"TaskVal" conform, so #~"spawn", #~"spawnVal",
#~"spawnDetached" and #~"JoinHandle.create" are all crossings. A protocol of
your own declares it the same way -- the job protocol of a pool whose own code
starts threads, say -- and its callers are then checked at the call:

#code language=zerolang: """
  # Job -- work a pool runs on one of its threads
  Job: protocol {
      # the work
      run: function {:this}
  } as {
      crossing: thread.Crossing
  }

  # submit -- hand `job` to the pool (here it runs at once)
  submit: function {job: J.take} is {
      job.run
  } as {
      J: Job.generic
  }

  # Count -- a job that counts its items
  Count: class {
      # the items
      items: ListVal i64
  } as {
      job: Job
      run: function {:this} is { print "counted \{this.items.length}" }
  }

  main: function is {
      xs: ListVal i64
      xs.append 1
      xs.append 2
      xs.append 3
      submit job: (Count items: xs.take)
  }
"""

Only a protocol declares #~"thread.Crossing"; a class that does is refused.

#--: Panics

A panic on any thread ends the whole program, with status #~"1": the panic is
reported once, however many threads panic, and the process ends without running
exit handlers under the other threads' feet. There is no catching it and no
poisoned state to recover.

#--: Building

A program that starts a thread links #~"pthread" (the #~"zlink:" line of its C
says so), and its C differs from a program that does not only where threads
need it -- the panic above. Programs that start no thread compile exactly as
they did. The C layer is #~"src/runtime/natives/_Z_THREAD_SHIM.inc": pthreads
on Linux and other POSIX systems, Win32 threads on Windows.
